  General methodology: The global approach considered in this    
          work is presented in the following papers:             
          (1) Modern nuclear data evaluation with the TALYS      
              code system, A.J. Koning and D. Rochman,           
              A.J. Koning and D. Rochman,                        
              Nuclear Data Sheets 113 (2012) 2841.               
          (2) TENDL: Complete Nuclear Data Library for           
              Innovative Nuclear Science and Technology,         
              A.J. Koning, D. Rochman, J.Ch Sublet, N. Dzysiuk,  
              M. Fleming and S. van der Marck,                   
              Nuclear Data Sheets 155 (2019) 1.                  
                                                                 
  MF2:  Resolved resonance range  (RRR)                          
       The RRR was generated with TARES-1.8 , compiled on         
       Oct  7 2026, 09:32:19 at Villigen   , Switzerland. The RRR
       expands from 0 to 4.975198E+1 eV. A total of 
       2 l-values are used and 548 resonances. The resonance 
       formalism is multi-level Breit Wigner (MLBW).
       The TARES database for the measured resonances is         
        ->  default up to 0.283377 eV.                                          
       Above this energy, the ladder approach is used.           
       The ladder approach from the CALENDF code is used to      
       generate statistical resonances in the unresolved         
       resonance range. Therefore, the URR is translated into    
       resolved resonance range. Explanations about the method   
       can be found in the CALENDF manual: CEA-Report CEA-R-6277 
       "CALENDF-2010: User manual" by J.C. Sublet, P. Ribon and
       M. Coste-Delcaux.                                         
       The method of creating statistical resonances in the      
       URR region is described in: "From average parameters to  
       statistical resolved resonances", D. Rochman et al.,     
       Annals of Nucl. Ene. 51 (2013) 60.                        
       The s-wave average level spacing is 0.283377 eV and  
       the s-wave neutron strength is 0.000034 1e-4.        
                                                                 
  MF33 in the resonance range:                                   
  The MF33 in the resonance range is obtained by using a number  
  of MF2 random files. There is therefore a consistency          
  between the MF2 random and the MF33.                           
  MF32:                                                          
  The MF32 contains the resonance parameters, uncertainties and  
  some correlation. These values are used to generate random     
  MF2, taking advantage of the code ENDSAM from L. Plevnik and   
  G. Zerovnik from JSI, Slovenia (thanks to A. Trkov, IAEA).     
                                                                 
                                                                 
